Title:
SOCKSRelayd — SOCKS-focused NTLM relay with persistent session packages and a SessionBank daemon

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
SOCKSRelayd is an NTLM relay tool (in the spirit of `ntlmrelayx -socks`) that persists relay state across operator shell exits by splitting responsibilities between an on-disk “package” store and a long-lived background “SessionBank” daemon. It addresses the common issue where quitting `ntlmrelayx` loses in-memory NTLM spoof material and live relayed sessions even though SMB keepalives could maintain them. The SessionBank owns authenticated TCP/SMB connections and keepalives, while serialized NTLM artifacts (e.g., challenge messages, captured NetNTLM material) are stored in a session directory for later reloading. Operators can detach/reattach to the running bank, stop/start the SOCKS frontend independently, and maintain an inventory of sessions via an append-only index. It’s primarily useful for red teams and pentesters doing NTLM relay and post-relay pivoting via SOCKS/proxychains. It’s interesting because it operationalizes “relay session persistence” and separates long-lived network state from interactive tooling, improving reliability during long engagements.

Technical Focus:
- NTLM relay over SMB/HTTP (listener/relay workflow)
- Persistent serialization of NTLM session artifacts (“packages”)
- Long-lived daemon managing authenticated TCP connections + SMB keepalives
- SOCKS proxy frontend for post-relay pivoting (proxychains integration)
- IPC control channel (bank socket) and detach/reattach operator workflow

Use Cases:
- Maintain relayed SMB sessions for hours/days without keeping an interactive `ntlmrelayx` process attached
- Detach from a relay box (or lose a shell) and later reattach to reuse still-live authenticated connections
- Run SOCKS-based pivoting to access internal SMB services using relayed auth (`smbclient.py -no-pass` via proxychains)
- Collect and retain an indexed inventory of captured NTLM relay artifacts and session metadata for later analysis
- Operate relay infrastructure under process supervision (tmux/supervisord) using daemon mode

Keywords:
NTLM relay, SMB, HTTP relay, SOCKS proxy, ntlmrelayx, Impacket, NetNTLM, Type 3 message, CHALLENGE_MESSAGE, keepalive, authenticated TCP session, session persistence, daemon, IPC socket, proxychains, pivoting, session serialization, relay-sessions, red team tooling, post-exploitation